{"kind": "report", "step": 18, "ts": "2026-11-19T15:28:16.121484+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit", "cause": "dns1.lan's resolver rate-limits the whole 10.20.0.0/24 to 20 q/s (ratelimit: 20, ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24), so bursts from clients are occasionally dropped; the client then falls back to its second server gw.lan, whose unbound has private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 rebinding protection that discards the 10.20.0.50 answer coming back through the \"lan.\" forward-zone \u2014 a direct test shows gw.lan answers nas.lan with NOERROR and an empty answer section, which is exactly what makes getaddrinfo return ENOTFOUND until the next successful retry against dns1 clears it.", "evidence": ["mon.lan /etc/resolv.conf: nameserver 10.20.0.3 first, nameserver 10.20.0.1 second, search lan \u2014 clients use both servers", "dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan on mon.lan: status NOERROR, ANSWER: 1, nas.lan 10 IN A 10.20.0.50 \u2014 dns1 resolves local names correctly", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan on mon.lan: status NOERROR but ANSWER: 0, AUTHORITY: 0 (empty answer, MSG SIZE 36 vs 52) \u2014 gw.lan returns 'no such name' style empty answer for the same name; repeated from gw.lan itself with drill, same empty NOERROR", "dns1.lan /opt/primary-dns/config.yaml lines 4-7: upstream_dns quad9 DoT, ratelimit: 20, ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24, refuse_any: true \u2014 one shared token bucket for the entire /24 explains brief, self-clearing outages and why public-name queries through the same path are not affected in a lasting way", "gw.lan /var/unbound/unbound.conf lines 15-18: 'DNS rebinding protection' private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 and 127.0.0.0/8, while lines 20-22 forward-zone \"lan.\" to 10.20.0.3 \u2014 the forwarded answer 10.20.0.50 is stripped as a private address, so the fallback server cannot serve any .lan host", "gw.lan service unbound status: running (pid 71204); dns1.lan ps aux: primary-dns pid 402 running \u2014 neither resolver is crashed, matching alerts that clear within a minute or two"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Fix both ends of the local-name path: on gw.lan, exempt the forwarded \"lan.\" zone from the private-address rebinding filter (e.g., declare lan. with a type that keeps the data, such as a tag/local-zone override, or apply private-address only to the internet-facing forward via view/tag), so the backup server can answer .lan names; and on dns1.lan, raise ratelimit (or per-client accounting) above the burst level seen from the /24 so normal client bursts are not dropped. The minimal fix for the alerts themselves is making gw.lan return the .lan records, since that turns every rate-limit-triggered fallback into a successful lookup.", "detail": "rate limiter on primary-dns intermittently drops client queries", "contributing": {"host": "gw.lan", "component": "dns_resolver_config"}}}
